rything to 0o700, then calls `shutil.rmtree(path, ignore_errors=True)`"],"finding":"Part of the plan's reason for not reusing `_rmtree_writable` is wrong. The plan says importing `rebar.llm` 'would pull the agents layer into a stdlib-only module'. But `workspace.py` is not stdlib-only (it imports `rebar.config` and `rebar._snapshot.git_fetch`), and the op-cert service already imports `rebar.llm` lazily in `jobs.py`. Not reusing the helper is still correct, but for different reasons: it is a private helper in another layer's module, and its semantics differ (it pre-walks the tree and chmods everything to 0o700 for a tree deliberately set read-only, then still calls `ignore_errors=True`).","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f737057f0c137129e","impact":0.55,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach — bullet 'The repo's other helper, `llm/workflow/snapshot.py::_rmtree_writable` … is not reused'","norm_id":"n2c5415df34899ac9","priority":0.3438,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A later reader or reviewer sees that jobs.py already imports rebar.llm, decides the stated reason is false, and 'fixes' it by importing the private `_rmtree_writable` across layers."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Replace the reason with: \"It is not reused: it is a private helper of `llm/workflow/snapshot.py`, built for a tree that module deliberately chmodded to 0o555. It walks the whole tree up front and then still calls `rmtree(ignore_errors=True)`. Reaching into another layer's private helper from the op-cert workspace (which jobs.py imports only lazily) is not worth it for a ~10-line rmtree error hook.\"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.625,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"no","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"no","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"na","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"incomplete_enumeration","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.75,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Rewrite the reason for not reusing `_rmtree_writable`: workspace.py is not stdlib-only. The real constraint is that `discard` must not import the optional `rebar.llm` package (the [agents] extra; see jobs.py `_llm_error_classes`).","cohort":["E4"],"criteria":["E4"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.4062,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: \"importing `rebar.llm` into the op-cert service would pull the agents layer into a stdlib-only module.\"","src/rebar/opcert_service/workspace.py:16-18 imports `rebar.config`, `rebar._snapshot.git_fetch.stall_abort_args` and `rebar.opcert_service.config`; line 143 lazily imports `rebar._store.ensures.run_ensures`, so the module is not stdlib-only.","src/rebar/opcert_service/jobs.py:175, 183, 191 and 207 already import `rebar.llm` lazily. `_llm_error_classes` (jobs.py:203-215) falls back to a sentinel when `rebar.llm` is unavailable (\"no `[agents]` extra\"), so the op-cert service already depends on rebar.llm, guarded as optional."],"finding":"The reason given for not reusing `_rmtree_writable` rests on a false premise. It calls `rebar.opcert_service.workspace` a \"stdlib-only module\", but workspace.py already imports several non-stdlib rebar packages. The decision may still be right, since `rebar.llm` is optional (the [agents] extra) and jobs.py guards its imports for that case, but the plan states the wrong reason.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fcbf194cf12d4e085","impact":0.55,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach — bullet on `llm/workflow/snapshot.py::_rmtree_writable` not being reused","norm_id":"n8ef3ec885504a7e5","priority":0.3438,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A later reviewer or implementer reads \"stdlib-only\" as a real constraint and rejects a legitimate in-repo import such as rebar._io or a shared helper module, or treats the module's existing rebar imports as a violation to fix.","Someone moves `_rmtree_writable` to a non-llm shared module. The stated premise then no longer argues against reuse, and the real reason (`rebar.llm` is optional, so `discard` must not depend on it) is not on record."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Replace \"would pull the agents layer into a stdlib-only module\" with: \"importing it would make `discard`, which runs on every job including the cleanup path in `prepare_workspace`, depend on the optional `rebar.llm` package ([agents] extra), which jobs.py deliberately imports only lazily and guarded.\"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.625,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"no","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"no","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"na","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"incomplete_enumeration","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] In `_retry_writable`, chmod directories (func is os.rmdir) with `stat.S_IRWXU` (0o700) and files with `S_IWRITE | S_IREAD`, and correct the 'never left at 0o200' / 'POSIX unchanged' rationale to match.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.6,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'runs os.chmod(path, stat.S_IWRITE | stat.S_IREAD) on the path that failed … Adding the read bit means a directory is never left at 0o200.'","Plan: 'On POSIX … behaviour there is unchanged apart from one extra chmod attempt after a failure.'","stat.S_IWRITE|stat.S_IREAD == 0o600; a POSIX directory needs S_IEXEC to be traversed and its entries removed.","Compare snapshot.py::_rmtree_writable, which uses 0o700 (it keeps the execute bit)."],"finding":"The hook chmods the failing path to `stat.S_IWRITE | stat.S_IREAD` (0o600) whether it is a file or a directory. On POSIX, a directory at 0o600 has no execute bit, so its owner can no longer get into it. The plan says 'Adding the read bit means a directory is never left at 0o200' and 'behaviour there is unchanged apart from one extra chmod attempt'. Both are wrong for POSIX directories. When os.rmdir fails on POSIX (for example ENOTEMPTY because a child could not be removed, or a parent-dir permission problem), the hook narrows the leftover directory to 0o600. Later cleanup by an operator or by `rm -rf` then fails until someone chmods it again.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f09153f16cd3201fc","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach — hook bullet ('os.chmod(path, stat.S_IWRITE | stat.S_IREAD) … a directory is never left at 0o200') and POSIX bullet ('behaviour there is unchanged')","norm_id":"n0abaf2898872498c","priority":0.0,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["On a POSIX host, a nested dir under the workspace has a child that fails to unlink. rmdir on the dir fails with ENOTEMPTY, the hook sets the dir to 0o600, and the leftover dir cannot be traversed by its owner."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Choose the mode by func: `mode = stat.S_IRWXU if func is os.rmdir else (stat.S_IWRITE | stat.S_IREAD)`. Then reword the bullet to: 'directories get owner rwx, files owner rw, so nothing is left untraversable.'","tier":"LLM","validity":0.8889,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"no","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"yes","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Declare depends_on edges to plump-smelly-indianabat and flowered-nonvalued-mink, or rewrite AC2 so it is evaluable for this ticket alone (e.g. a pre-merge sweep (windows) run on a branch carrying all three fixes).","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5b","T5e","T9"],"criteria":["E6","F1"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.6,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'After merge: once this, plump-smelly-indianabat (which re-enables the test on Windows) and flowered-nonvalued-mink are all on main, the leak test passes in a `sweep (windows)` run on main.'","No depends_on / blocks relation to those tickets is stated anywhere in the plan."],"finding":"AC2 can only pass once two other tickets (plump-smelly-indianabat, which re-enables the test on Windows, and flowered-nonvalued-mink) are on main. The plan declares no dependency edge on either, so this ticket may not be closable in-session and could sit open indefinitely, or be closed without its AC met.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fac6c1c057e9fff34","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria — AC2; Testing — 'After merge' bullet","norm_id":"n4a53842e0efc9c9b","priority":0.0,"reason":"veto:absence-refuted","recalled":false,"scenarios":["This ticket merges first: on main the leak test is still disabled on Windows, so AC2 cannot be evaluated.","flowered-nonvalued-mink slips: AC2 fails for reasons unrelated to this change."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Add the dependency edges. Also add a pre-merge proving step: rerun the `sweep (windows)` diag on the `w1-diag-opcert-acl` branch with this fix applied, and record the run id showing the leak test passing. Keep the main-branch run as a follow-up confirmation.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.2857,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"no","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"na","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State explicitly whether every leftover file in run 44593555306 was read-only. If any were not, name their failure mode and say whether the hook handles it.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5b","T5e","T9"],"criteria":["E2","T2"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.6,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'323 of the remaining files were read-only, all under `<ws>\\.git\\objects\\..`.'"],"finding":"The Observation says '323 of the remaining files were read-only'. That phrasing leaves open whether other, writable files were also left behind. If they were, read-only attributes do not explain the whole leak (e.g. files locked by a running git process), and the chmod-and-retry hook would not fix it.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fda106b12b10b2c1a","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Observation","norm_id":"ncec0e0aab0c7080c","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Some leftovers are writable but locked. After the fix, AC2 still fails and the cause has to be diagnosed again."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.25,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"insufficient","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"insufficient","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"none","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] In the Observation, state whether every leftover entry was either a read-only file under .git/objects or a dir that held one, or name the other cause and cover it in the Approach.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.6,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'The sandbox TMPDIR … still held 13 or more rebar-opcert-ws-* job workspaces. 323 of the remaining files were read-only, all under <ws>\\.git\\objects\\..'","Plan Cause attributes the whole leak to read-only git objects. It does not account for any remaining entries that are not read-only.","Current code: `shutil.rmtree(root, ignore_errors=True)` (workspace.py:158). It keeps going after each per-file failure, so only undeletable files and their ancestor dirs should remain."],"finding":"The Observation says only that '323 of the remaining files were read-only, all under <ws>\\.git\\objects'. That wording implies other files remained that were not read-only. On Windows, rmtree with ignore_errors still tries every file, so the read-only cause alone does not explain why non-read-only files survived. If any did, something else is also blocking deletion, for example handles held by a lingering git child process or by the tracker worktree. The chmod-and-retry hook would not fix that, and AC2 would fail after merge. The plan does not show whether every remaining entry is explained by the read-only cause.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fc80a520416d56a8c","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Observation / Cause","norm_id":"nddef40042a47c2e4","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Some of the surviving files are locked by an open handle rather than marked read-only. The fix removes the read-only objects, but the workspace dir still remains, and the leak test still fails on `sweep (windows)`."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.25,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"insufficient","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"insufficient","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"none","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Add an unpatched test that makes files read-only with os.chmod(path, stat.S_IREAD), calls discard, and asserts the tree is gone, and confirm it runs in the Windows CI job before merge.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5b","T5e","T9"],"criteria":["E6","T2"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":"novelty","evidence":["'`os.unlink` is patched with a fake that ... raises `PermissionError` while the target lacks `S_IWRITE`'","'All tests run on every platform and model Windows semantics in the test, without relying on real POSIX directory permissions'"],"finding":"Every planned unit test patches os.unlink with a fake that simulates the Windows refusal, including when the suite runs on Windows. So before merge, nothing proves the hook works against Windows' actual read-only behaviour. The real Windows unlink, os.chmod(S_IWRITE) clearing the read-only attribute, and rmdir of the parent are only exercised by the post-merge AC2 run.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f7df92b105b92626a","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Testing — first bullet; 'All tests run on every platform and model Windows semantics in the test'","norm_id":"nc7ae72cdaacc22c6","priority":0.0,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["On Windows, removal also fails with a sharing violation (e.g. a lingering git process holds an object file open). The fake-based test is green, but the real workspace still leaks.","The fake's stat-based S_IWRITE check diverges from how Windows maps the read-only attribute, so the test passes on a mismatched model."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Add `test_discard_removes_real_readonly_files`: build dirs/files mirroring .git/objects/xx/<sha>, run os.chmod(f, stat.S_IREAD) on each file, call discard, and assert not root.exists(). Do not patch os.unlink. On Windows this reproduces the real refusal; on POSIX it is a harmless regression check.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.625,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] After rmtree returns, if the root still exists (or the except branch fires), log one warning naming the workspace path, without raising, and cover it with a test using the always-fail unlink fake.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5b","T5e","T9"],"criteria":["T5b"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":"novelty","evidence":["'any `OSError` from either step is swallowed'","'The whole `rmtree` call is also wrapped in `try/except Exception`, so `discard` stays best-effort'"],"finding":"discard swallows every failure — OSError inside the hook, plus any Exception around rmtree — and emits nothing. If a workspace still cannot be removed, operators get no signal. That silent swallow is exactly how this leak went unnoticed.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f3a4d37c4a3b6a345","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach — hook and try/except bullets","norm_id":"n285b6a152c7c46c6","priority":0.0,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A new Windows failure mode (locked file, long path) leaks workspaces again, and nothing is visible until a sweep test happens to catch it."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"After the rmtree call: `if os.path.lexists(root): logger.warning('op-cert workspace not fully removed: %s', root)`. In the `except Exception` branch, log the same warning with exc_info. Assert the warning in the 'os.unlink always raises' test via caplog.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.75,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation